#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175670 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"}] :question/id #uuid "ef520918-4e4b-516b-acaa-57a14b5c1405" :question/math-academy-id "e-16134" :question/problem "Calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n$x = \\big\\{6, \\: -2, \\: 7, \\: 1 \\big\\},\\qquad y = \\big\\{5, \\: -1, \\: 4, \\: 0 \\big\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y})\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\n$$\n\nare the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (6 + (-2) + 7 + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 3 \\\\\ny^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (5 + (-1) + 4 + 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov(x, y) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(6 - 3)(5 - 2) + (-2 - 3)(-1 - 2) + (7 - 3)(4 - 2) + (1 - 3)(0 - 2)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(3)(3) + (-5)(-3) + (4)(2) + (-2)(-2)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[9 + 15 + 8 + 4] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 36 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175671 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "33adfe2b-297e-5c52-8c4f-088d0c2b66e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16136" :question/problem "A math teacher asked four of her students how many hours they had slept the night before an exam. The information she received is shown in the table above. Compute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overline{x}=5$ and $\\overline{y}=73$.\n\n| **Number of Hours of Sleep ($x$)** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Points Scored ($y$)** | $54$ | $68$ | $80$ | $90$ |" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y})\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\n$$\n\nare the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov(x, y) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(2 - 5)(54 - 73) + (4 - 5)(68 - 73) + (6 - 5)(80 - 73) + (8 - 5)(90 - 73)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(-19) + (-1)(-5) + (1)(7) + (3)(17)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[57 + 5 + 7 + 51] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9479bcac-f2e2-54e8-871a-0d853dfc3208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174193} {:answer/id #uuid "d761946c-ef86-5b2e-a53e-93beef450140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174194} {:answer/id #uuid "180fdc3d-4903-54c0-ab8f-7461c6609a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174195} {:answer/id #uuid "3f64d110-e09b-5793-9342-f50897afc43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174196} {:answer/id #uuid "52faad75-5450-5cb0-88f5-cf3a47cb425f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d761946c-ef86-5b2e-a53e-93beef450140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174194} :answer-field/id #uuid "3983408a-0e4a-5aa4-9444-ac9d3488dcb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174192}] :question/id #uuid "9b3a2205-203a-5056-a951-45e86cc0afea" :question/math-academy-id "q-134066" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following data sets:\n$x = {4,-1, 0, 5}, y = {6, 3, 12, 11}$\nYou're given that $x^(―) = 2$ and $y^(―) = 8$. Calculate $Cov(x, y)$." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "886af3dd-5d8b-5ea6-bab2-129112bd4cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174199} {:answer/id #uuid "8ede0f34-48ea-5d8e-a803-e12856c5dea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174200} {:answer/id #uuid "b6175ddc-549a-5d8c-8d2d-76cfbfa8af72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174201} {:answer/id #uuid "bef32139-a042-5774-8261-dadd61470199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174202} {:answer/id #uuid "00a79302-e254-5a84-80eb-2c282e9f701a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answe